<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mont Ventoux</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mont Ventoux is a mountain in Provence, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, rising to 1,910 metres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, the highest peak in the region. Its bare limestone summit is visible from far across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> described climbing it in 1336, and it is famous as one of the hardest climbs of the Tour de France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mont-ventoux.jpg" alt="Mont Ventoux" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: muneaki · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mont_Ventoux_090927.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
